      "id": 1,
      "text": "In 1828, the settlement of Savageville was renamed Providence when its post office opened, a change that honored settlers’ gratitude for divine protection.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Providence%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Richard Savage, a Virginian, opened a general store that became the nucleus of Providence, the city that grew around his shop.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Providence%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Commercial coal mining started in 1888, and every June Providence celebrates its mining heritage with an annual Coal Festival.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Providence%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1993, the Providence Commercial Historic District was created to honor the historic downtown buildings that define the city’s architectural heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Providence%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Situated in Kentucky’s Black Patch tobacco region, Providence rose to become the third‑largest stemming market in the United States.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Providence%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Tradewater River runs just west of Providence, and the city centers around the crossroads of Kentucky Routes 109 and 120.",
